424. **The six Vouch stories worth building, and the one rule we keep** — from `docs/research/vouch-user-stories.md` (`3c14ca9`): 24 stories across six actors, each carrying what the product
    allows, what a rule blocks, and whether the block is real or self-imposed. **Our rules block almost none of it**; the binding constraints are real only where they need a bank (tap-to-pay,
    BIN) or custody (escrow, refunds — a principle worth keeping).
    - **build order**: (1) **agent proves authority / counterparty scans** — one endpoint, `#423`; (2) **seller preflight via `X-Vouch-Serial`**, which makes Vouch something sellers want rather
      than a buyer ornament; (3) **single-use pass** (*"this agent may pay this seller once, $5, today"*) — the clearest demo of understanding, and it needs a `consumed` state; (4)
      **sub-mandate chain** — an agent hires a second agent under a narrower cap, which nobody in the market does and is nearly free once the state machine exists; (5) **revoke whole or per
      agent**; (6) **point-in-time verification** (*"was this valid at 14:02 on 3 Feb"*), which is what makes it evidence rather than a status light.
    - **the sketch gaps the stories expose**: a mandate may cover **several agents** (one serial family, per-pass revocation), a pass may be **single-use**, and **a seller-side subscription to
      revocations** is a feature nobody else offers.
    - **the rule we keep, decided 2026-11-24: no account layer.** The researcher is right that a key-held, read-only mandate index would unlock three enterprise stories with no custody — but
      *"no account, no email: an agent cannot fill in a signup form"* is a headline claim we would have to weaken, and an index of mandates you issued is a new identity surface by definition.
      **We keep the claim and those three stories wait.** If a customer ever pays for the admin panel, that is the moment to revisit it — with the privacy page rewritten first, not after. · M

    - **revision 2 of the concept paper corrects the pitch, and it is a correction we must carry everywhere**: **the cap is advisory today** — our gate cannot stop an agent holding an
      unrestricted wallet, because it can skip the call and sign. So "a cap it cannot exceed" is only true of a compliant agent. Every claim now names an **enforcement level**:
      **advisory** (today: client + gate, *"a cap a compliant agent respects"*), **session-key** (smart-account key with an on-chain cap: *"a cap it cannot exceed"*), or **policy-proxy**
      (a wallet that refuses to sign without a verdict: *"cannot exceed, by construction"*). The mandate names its level and the pass says which — that is the difference between a safety
      product and a suggestion.
    - **and the buyer is probably not the seller**: x402 settles on-chain with no chargebacks, so a seller has little reason to check who authorised a payment that already cleared. **The
      operator and the enterprise are the customer**; seller verification is for high-value or disputed calls. The seller question therefore moves first as the cheapest kill — *"for a $50
      job, or a dispute later, would your server check a mandate URL?"*
    - **cheap fixes accepted**: the verdict binds **a digest of the exact challenge with a TTL** (else an agent swaps the payment after approval); a **revocation-race rule** (revoke after
      the verdict, before signing → the receipt flags it rather than pretending); **EXHAUSTED split from CONSUMED** (six states); every name marked **`self-asserted`**, domain proof
      optional and never implied KYC; "10 agents via operators" corrected to **10 operators**.

425. **A signer-side adapter that fails closed — the only thing that makes the cap real** — all four reviews of `docs/research/opportunity-synthesis.md` (`a4c570b`) converge on
    the same first move, and it is also the fix for our own concept paper's flaw: **the cap is advisory until something at the signing boundary refuses.** The adapter submits the exact
    payment, verifies our receipt, stops on `do_not_pay`, **fails closed when we are unreachable**, and **binds the verdict to the payment digest** so the challenge cannot be swapped after
    approval.
    - done when: an adapter exists for at least one signer (viem/ethers account or a smart-account session key) that (a) refuses to sign on `do_not_pay`, (b) refuses to sign when the gate
      is unreachable, (c) refuses when the challenge digest differs from the one the verdict covers, and (d) has a test for each of those three, plus one where a conforming payment passes.
    - **what it changes**: the honest claim moves from *"a cap a compliant agent respects"* (advisory) to *"a cap it cannot exceed"* — the session-key/policy-proxy level in `#424`.
    - **the number that decides everything after it**: **non-own callers and non-own downloads.** If the adapter and verifier ship and that number stays flat, the honest conclusion is not
      more features — it is that an independent gate is not adopted unless something forces it, and the pivot is the public data layer alone.
    · gate: none · M

